add(SECTIONS, 210, 'ICSI, sperm retrieval, treatment table and viability tests', '''
fillup | ICSI introduces ____ directly into the ____ of each oocyte. | One sperm; cytoplasm | 50,000 sperm; perivitelline culture dish | One embryo; cervical mucus | One spermatid; uterine myometrium | Intracytoplasmic injection uses one selected sperm per oocyte; collection is otherwise described as similar to IVF.
odd | Which is outside the page's ICSI indication trio? | Mild oligospermia at 12 million/mL with adequate motility | Severe oligospermia <5 million/mL | Azoospermia with retrievable sperm | Asthenospermia with motility <5% | The source reserves ICSI for severe count or movement problems and azoospermia with usable sperm.
fillup | The severe motility threshold listed for ICSI is ____. | <5% | <30% | <42% | <54% | The threshold in the ICSI block is much lower than the reference total-motility value.
scenario | Testicular azoospermia has high FSH/LH and low testosterone. Why perform testicular biopsy in the source pathway? | To find retrievable testicular sperm for possible ICSI | To establish distal duct obstruction from tissue fructose levels | To replace all endocrine and genetic assessment with morphology alone | To confirm that normal serum FSH excludes a production defect | If sperm are found, the diagram proceeds to surgical extraction and ICSI; diagnostic and retrieval planning require specialist judgement.
match | Match 1) TESA 2) TESE with A) Testicular sperm extraction B) Testicular sperm aspiration. | 1-B, 2-A | 1-A, 2-B | Both A | Both B | Aspiration and extraction distinguish the two testicular retrieval abbreviations.
match | Match 1) PESA 2) MESA with A) Microsurgical epididymal sperm aspiration B) Percutaneous epididymal sperm aspiration. | 1-B, 2-A | 1-A, 2-B | Both A | Both B | Both are epididymal, but percutaneous and microsurgical approaches differ.
scenario | Use the source management table: concentration is 12 million/mL, tubes are patent and other criteria fit. Which method is selected? | IUI | Conventional IVF as the only option | ICSI as the only option | Sildenafil solely for the sperm count | The table uses 10–15 million/mL for IUI; the earlier indication page uses 10–16.
scenario | Use the same management table: sperm concentration is 7 million/mL. Which method is listed? | IVF | IUI | HRT | Vasectomy reversal irrespective of anatomy | Five to ten million/mL is the IVF row in this simplified treatment table.
scenario | Use the management table: sperm concentration is 3 million/mL. Which method is listed? | ICSI | IUI | Clomiphene alone in the female partner | Hysteroscopic cannulation | Less than five million/mL is directed to ICSI in the source.
fillup | Non-obstructive azoospermia is managed in the table with ____ followed by ____, if sperm are obtainable. | Surgical sperm extraction; ICSI | Urine collection alone; HSG | Huhner testing; IUI regardless of sperm presence | Vasectomy; conventional insemination | Successful surgical retrieval provides sperm for ICSI; retrieval is not guaranteed.
match | Match 1) Erectile dysfunction 2) Premature ejaculation with A) SSRI B) Sildenafil. | 1-B, 2-A | 1-A, 2-B | Both A | Both B | The table separates erection treatment from ejaculatory-delay treatment.
scenario | Hypospadias prevents effective semen delivery despite usable sperm. Which treatment is shown? | IUI | HRT | Salpingectomy | AMH replacement | IUI bypasses the delivery problem when the partner's tubes and other prerequisites are suitable.
fillup | In retrograde ejaculation, the table retrieves sperm from ____ for ____. | Urine; IUI | Cervical mucus; IVF | Seminal-vesicle tissue; HSG | Endometrium; ICSI | Sperm recovery from postejaculatory urine can permit assisted insemination after appropriate preparation.
fillup | The two supravital stains named for distinguishing live and dead sperm are ____. | Eosin and trypan blue | Methylene blue and indigo carmine | Haematoxylin and periodic acid–Schiff | Lugol iodine and acetic acid | Eosin/trypan blue staining assesses membrane integrity, unlike the dyes used for tubal assessment.
match | Match supravital-stain result: 1) Stained 2) Unstained with A) Live B) Dead. | 1-B, 2-A | 1-A, 2-B | Both A | Both B | Dead sperm take up the stain because membrane integrity is lost.
scenario | An immobile sperm swells in a hypotonic osmotic test. What does that indicate? | It is live despite immobility | It is definitely dead | It has no membrane | It is an epithelial cell by definition | Swelling indicates membrane responsiveness and viability; absence of movement is not proof of death.
fillup | In the hypotonic swelling test, no swelling is associated in the source with ____ sperm. | Dead | Live and motile | Live but immobile in every case | Normally fertilising | The page contrasts no swelling/dead with swelling/live immobile sperm.
fillup | The myeloperoxidase assay for round cells in semen is also called the ____ test. | Endtz | Huhner | Rubin | Spinnbarkeit | Endtz helps identify peroxidase-positive leukocytes among round cells.
match | Match source Endtz interpretation: 1) Positive 2) Negative with A) Round sperm/germ-cell interpretation B) WBC. | 1-B, 2-A | 1-A, 2-B | Both A | Both B | Positive supports leukocytes. The source calls negative cells round sperm without an acrosomal cap; peroxidase negativity alone is not a definitive cell diagnosis.
''')
